  "seoBody": "<h2>Read this first: these are not the same tool</h2><p>We want to be straight about this from the start. FleetCheck is a full fleet-management system. Quickler is not. FleetCheck tracks MOT and tax due dates and sends reminders, holds driver records, manages defect workflows from report through to repair, and gives a fleet manager a whole-fleet compliance view. Quickler does none of that.</p><p>Quickler only does the on-site capture piece: the driver's daily DVSA walkaround, done in WhatsApp, with photos of any defects, into a timestamped report. So the honest way to think about a FleetCheck alternative is to first decide whether you need fleet management, or just want drivers to complete the daily walkaround.</p><h2>What FleetCheck does that Quickler does not</h2><p>The gap is large and worth stating plainly. FleetCheck is a fleet-management platform. It reminds you about MOT and tax. It holds driver records, licences and CPC. It manages defects from report to repair. It gives a whole-fleet view of compliance in one place. FleetCheck also includes a driver app for the walkaround check.</p><p>Quickler does not remind you about an MOT, does not hold a driver record, does not track a defect to repair, and does not manage a fleet. If those jobs are your problem, FleetCheck is the right category of product and quickler is not a substitute.</p><h2>The one narrow problem Quickler solves</h2><p>The daily DVSA driver walkaround check is a legal duty. On a cold, dark morning with a schedule to hit, it is the thing that gets skipped or filled in later from memory.</p><p>FleetCheck includes a driver app for this, and if your drivers use it, that is a solved problem. But an app is a download, a login, one more thing to open when hands are cold and the day is starting. When drivers stop opening it, the record says the check was done. It was done in the cab, later, from memory. That is not a compliant record. It is the appearance of one.</p><p>That gap, drivers not completing the walkaround because the app is friction, is the one thing quickler is for.</p><h2>The alternatives</h2><h3>FleetCheck</h3><p>The full fleet-management system: reminders, driver records, defect management, whole-fleet admin, plus a driver app for the walkaround. Choose it when you need to manage the fleet, not just capture the check.</p><h3>Paper defect books</h3><p>Still common. Free, familiar, no training. The records are illegible, unsearchable, and impossible to produce when DVSA asks. The cost is not the book. It is the liability when the completed check cannot be found.</p><h3>Quickler</h3><p>Quickler does only the capture. The walkaround arrives in the driver's existing WhatsApp chat, they reply to each check as a message and send photos of any defect, and it becomes a timestamped report. Voice notes are transcribed automatically. The output is a shareable web report and a permanent record, with optional PDF and CSV export. There is nothing to install and no login.</p><p>Pricing is only for active users: [[price:bundles]]. An active user is someone who completed at least two workflows that month, so you add every driver and only pay for who actually works. Everything is unlimited on a paid account.</p><p>The honest limitation, stated plainly: quickler is not fleet management. No MOT or tax reminders, no driver records, no defect-to-repair workflow, no whole-fleet dashboard. It is built for on-site capture only, for teams of roughly 1 to 30. Rooted in UK compliance, it works anywhere WhatsApp does. If you need to manage a fleet, keep FleetCheck.</p><h2>Direct comparison</h2><p><strong>FleetCheck:</strong> full fleet-management system; driver app and login required; yes, fleet management is the whole point.</p><p><strong>Paper defect book:</strong> manual daily check; no app; no fleet management.</p><p><strong>Quickler:</strong> on-site walkaround capture only; WhatsApp only, no install; no fleet management, capture only.</p><p>FleetCheck pricing is approximate and structured around fleet size and modules. Check their current page before deciding.</p><h2>How to choose</h2><p>The deciding question is not which is better, because they do different jobs.</p><p>If you need whole-fleet management, reminders, driver records and defect workflows in one place, FleetCheck is built for that and quickler does not replace it.</p><p>If your vehicles and admin are handled, but drivers keep skipping the daily walkaround because opening an app on a cold morning is one step too many, quickler removes the app and puts the walkaround in WhatsApp, because they already use it for everything else.</p><p>Some firms run both: FleetCheck for the admin, quickler for the capture.</p><p><strong>One thing to check:</strong> whatever you use, confirm the walkaround output meets DVSA record-keeping expectations for your operation.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Does Quickler replace FleetCheck?</h3><p>No. FleetCheck is a full fleet-management system: MOT and tax reminders, driver records, defect management, whole-fleet admin. Quickler only does the on-site capture: the driver's daily DVSA walkaround in WhatsApp. If you need fleet management, keep FleetCheck.</p><h3>What does Quickler actually do for fleet compliance?</h3><p>One thing, well. It puts the daily driver walkaround in WhatsApp, so the driver replies to each check as a message and sends photos of any defect, producing a timestamped report. There is no app to install and no login. It solves the problem of drivers skipping the walkaround because opening an app is friction. It does not manage the wider fleet.</p><h3>Can I use Quickler alongside FleetCheck?</h3><p>Yes, and some firms do. FleetCheck handles the fleet admin, reminders, records and defect workflows, while quickler handles the on-site walkaround capture in WhatsApp. They address different parts of the job.</p><h3>Is Quickler cheaper than FleetCheck?</h3><p>They are priced for different jobs, so a direct comparison is misleading. FleetCheck is priced around fleet size and modules; check their current page. Quickler is £20 per active user a month, where an active user is someone who completed at least two workflows that month, and dormant users are free. Quickler costs less because it does less: only the capture.</p><h2>Try this in Quickler</h2><p>Start free, no card, no trial clock. Copy and paste this into the workflow description when you sign up at <a href=\"https://app.quickler.co/signup\">app.quickler.co/signup</a>:</p><p><code>Driver walkaround check. Workflow name: Daily DVSA walkaround. Description: The daily driver walkaround check for our vehicles. Include the checks the driver runs (tyres, lights, mirrors, brakes, fluid levels, load security and so on), require a photo of any defect, and produce a timestamped report per vehicle per day. Quickler builds this as a WhatsApp workflow. £20 per active user a month, dormant users free, only pay for who works.</code></p><p>Quickler reads your description and builds the WhatsApp question sequence. Your drivers answer on site. Report produced automatically. This is capture only, not fleet management.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/goaudits-alternative-uk.html\">GoAudits alternative UK</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/iauditor-alternative-uk.html\">iAuditor alternative UK</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/lumiform-alternative-uk.html\">Lumiform alternative UK</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/alternatives.html\">All comparisons</a></li></ul>",
